---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Prose from "@/components/astro/Prose.astro";
import { SITE, PRIVACY_PROMISE } from "@/lib/site";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const lastUpdated = "November 17, 2026";
---

<PageLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="ChatWrapped's privacy policy: what data the app processes, what it stores, what it never sends, and what the hosting provider may record. Plain language, no dark patterns."
  path="/privacy/"
>
  <Prose />
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12">
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-bold text-white">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-zinc-500">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-zinc-300">
      ChatWrapped was built privacy-first: the entire application is a static site and all
      conversation processing happens in your browser. This policy explains exactly what that means
      — and what it doesn’t.
    </p>

    <div class="prose-cw">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <blockquote>{PRIVACY_PROMISE}</blockquote>
      <p>
        No account. No tracking cookies. No analytics scripts. No third-party requests during
        processing. No error reporting service. Nothing about your messages is stored after the tab
        closes unless you explicitly save a recap summary.
      </p>

      <h2>What the app processes, and where</h2>
      <p>
        When you import a chat export, the file is read with the browser’s File API and parsed in a
        Web Worker on your device. Parsing, statistics, story generation and card rendering all run
        locally. The app makes no network request that contains any part of your conversation — you
        can verify this in your browser’s network inspector at any time; our own automated audits
        check exactly this on every build.
      </p>

      <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Session memory (default):</strong> parsed conversations live in the tab’s memory and
          disappear when you close or reload it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Saved recaps (opt-in):</strong> if you choose “Save current recap summary”, a small
          aggregate record (message counts, date range, top emoji) is stored in your browser’s IndexedDB.
          Message text is never included.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Service worker cache:</strong> the app’s own static files are cached so it works offline.
          This is app code, not data.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deletion controls:</strong> the Privacy Center can delete any saved recap, all saved
          recaps, or the whole session at any time.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the hosting provider may process</h2>
      <p>
        ChatWrapped is hosted on GitHub Pages. Like any website, requesting the site’s static files
        exposes standard technical information (IP address, user agent, timestamps) to the hosting
        provider, which processes it under its own infrastructure policies. ChatWrapped adds nothing
        to that: there is no custom analytics, no logging of content, and no third-party script that
        could observe your visit. We do not claim that “absolutely nothing” is ever recorded by any
        infrastructure — that would be dishonest; we claim the app itself sends nothing beyond
        normal static-file requests.
      </p>

      <h2>What we never do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We never receive, store or transmit your chat files or message content.</li>
        <li>We never send conversation text to AI services or any other third party.</li>
        <li>We never put chat content in URLs.</li>
        <li>We never use cookies, fingerprinting, session recording or advertising.</li>
        <li>We never require an account, email address or payment.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Children’s privacy</h2>
      <p>
        ChatWrapped does not knowingly collect any personal data from anyone — there is simply no
        data collection to regulate. It is a tool you run on your own files.
      </p>

      <h2>Open source, verifiable</h2>
      <p>
        The entire application is open source at <a href={SITE.repo}>{SITE.repo}</a>. The privacy
        architecture is documented in the repository (<code
          >docs/privacy/PRIVACY_ARCHITECTURE.md</code
        >), and the automated browser audit that verifies “no chat content leaves the browser” is
        part of the test suite.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If the privacy architecture ever changes, this policy will change with it and the “last
        updated” date above will reflect that. We will never add data collection quietly.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions or a privacy concern? Open an issue at <a href={SITE.repo}>{SITE.repo}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</PageLayout>
